{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/a2-language-exchange-cafe/audio/main-7e9ea9fd376c.mp3","durationSec":351.096,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":4.18,"text":"A language exchange is a trade: you give something, and you get something back."},{"start":4.74,"end":7.52,"text":"You help someone with your language, and they help you with theirs."},{"start":8.26,"end":11.68,"text":"For example, you speak Japanese and want to practise English."},{"start":12.5,"end":15.28,"text":"So you look for an English speaker who wants to practise Japanese."},{"start":16.44,"end":19.02,"text":"Everyone is a teacher and a student at the same time."},{"start":19.68,"end":21.78,"text":"When you help with your language, you are the teacher."},{"start":22.5,"end":24.9,"text":"When the other person helps you, you are the student."},{"start":25.46,"end":29.04,"text":"In this lesson, you visit the Dutch Language Café in The Hague."},{"start":29.76,"end":31.44,"text":"The Hague is a city in the Netherlands."},{"start":32.2,"end":35.16,"text":"The café has a language exchange every Thursday evening."},{"start":36.06,"end":38.98,"text":"Every visitor gets a language sticker and wears it on their shirt."},{"start":39.7,"end":42.96,"text":"Write your languages on two lines, so people can see the difference."},{"start":43.82,"end":46.66,"text":"Your native language is the first language you learned as a child."},{"start":47.08,"end":48.22,"text":"It goes on the Speak line."},{"start":48.66,"end":52.26,"text":"For example:\n\nSpeak: Japanese."},{"start":52.26,"end":55.42,"text":"Practise: English."},{"start":55.42,"end":58.78,"text":"A sticker shows you who can help you."},{"start":59.48,"end":62.72,"text":"The room is full of strangers, people you do not know."},{"start":63.08,"end":65.4,"text":"But with one look at a sticker, you know their languages."},{"start":66.22,"end":67.96,"text":"It also gives you your first question."},{"start":68.9,"end":70.78,"text":"Do you see Spanish on the Practise line?"},{"start":71.58,"end":73.6,"text":"Ask: I see you study Spanish."},{"start":73.98,"end":74.24,"text":"Why?"},{"start":75.14,"end":79.52,"text":"The evening has a clear plan:\n\n19:30: arrive and get your sticker."},{"start":79.52,"end":84,"text":"20:00: speed exchange, 3 minutes with each person."},{"start":84,"end":88.6,"text":"20:30: language tables, with one language at each table."},{"start":88.6,"end":92.14,"text":"21:10: change tables if you want."},{"start":93.14,"end":96.34,"text":"In the speed exchange, you take turns choosing the language."},{"start":97.08,"end":101.84,"text":"First one of you chooses, then the other, so both people practise."},{"start":101.84,"end":104.08,"text":"After 3 minutes, you move to the next person."},{"start":104.92,"end":105.58,"text":"Why so short?"},{"start":106.34,"end":108.66,"text":"In a short time, you meet many new people."},{"start":109.32,"end":112.32,"text":"Your €11 ticket includes the snacks and the games."},{"start":112.86,"end":114.7,"text":"Included means they are in the price."},{"start":115.54,"end":118.04,"text":"The snacks are popcorn, crisps and nuts."},{"start":118.48,"end":119.32,"text":"You eat them at your table."},{"start":119.86,"end":122.42,"text":"Drinks are not included, so you buy them at the bar."},{"start":123.24,"end":126.56,"text":"You order from the bartender, the person who works behind the bar."},{"start":127.9,"end":128.94,"text":"You buy food there too."},{"start":129.74,"end":133.22,"text":"On the menu, you see a funny name: koffie verkeerd."},{"start":133.22,"end":135.32,"text":"In English, it means wrong coffee."},{"start":136.02,"end":136.56,"text":"Why wrong?"},{"start":137.18,"end":139.76,"text":"A normal Dutch coffee has only a little milk."},{"start":139.76,"end":144.12,"text":"Koffie verkeerd has a lot of warm milk, often more than the coffee."},{"start":144.58,"end":146.06,"text":"So it is the wrong way around."},{"start":146.96,"end":148.84,"text":"If you are hungry, try an empanada."},{"start":149.5,"end":151.86,"text":"It is small and baked, with food inside."},{"start":152.54,"end":155.54,"text":"At this café, you choose cheese, meat or spinach."},{"start":156.14,"end":158.44,"text":"When you do not understand, do not just say yes."},{"start":159.08,"end":161.7,"text":"A bar is loud, and a menu has new words."},{"start":162.46,"end":164.38,"text":"Ask: Sorry, can you say that again?"},{"start":164.92,"end":168.22,"text":"Or ask about one word: What does spinach mean?"},{"start":169,"end":172.78,"text":"At a language café, everyone is a student, so questions are normal."},{"start":173.66,"end":177.38,"text":"In the Netherlands, people shake hands with someone new and say their name."},{"start":178.06,"end":180.78,"text":"Often it is only the first name: Hi!"},{"start":181.22,"end":182.4,"text":"I'm Sanne."},{"start":182.4,"end":186.98,"text":"In Japan, many people bow, or bend forward, and say their family name."},{"start":187.54,"end":190.26,"text":"A hello without your name is rude, or not polite."},{"start":190.78,"end":191.02,"text":"Why?"},{"start":191.52,"end":193.34,"text":"Your name tells people who you are."},{"start":193.78,"end":195.98,"text":"It also tells them what to call you all evening."},{"start":196.54,"end":199.14,"text":"So when you hear a new name, say your name too."},{"start":199.94,"end":202.62,"text":"At a first meeting, do not ask: How are you?"},{"start":202.96,"end":207.08,"text":"For many Dutch people, it is a real question, not just a hello."},{"start":207.76,"end":210.14,"text":"They ask it when they really want to know the answer."},{"start":210.52,"end":213,"text":"So they usually ask people they already know."},{"start":213.84,"end":214.66,"text":"Save How are you?"},{"start":214.66,"end":217.06,"text":"for next Thursday, when you meet again."},{"start":217.64,"end":220.8,"text":"At a first meeting, just say hello and your name."},{"start":221.96,"end":223.42,"text":"A speed exchange is short."},{"start":223.9,"end":229.42,"text":"So say three things: your name, your city and why you study the language."},{"start":229.42,"end":231.66,"text":"Daan says: Hi!"},{"start":231.66,"end":232.46,"text":"I'm Daan."},{"start":232.46,"end":233.66,"text":"I live in The Hague."},{"start":233.66,"end":235.54,"text":"I study Japanese because I'm going to Tokyo."},{"start":236.42,"end":238.02,"text":"The reason is the most useful part."},{"start":238.68,"end":240.22,"text":"It gives the other person a question."},{"start":240.9,"end":243.18,"text":"For example: What do you want to see in Tokyo?"},{"start":244.16,"end":246.9,"text":"When you finish, ask back: What about you?"},{"start":247.62,"end":251.26,"text":"The best tool for a long conversation is the follow-up question."},{"start":251.96,"end":256.04,"text":"A follow-up question is a question about what the other person just said."},{"start":256.78,"end":258.2,"text":"It stays with their idea."},{"start":258.64,"end":263.36,"text":"A new question jumps to a new idea, for example: Do you like football?"},{"start":266.39,"end":272.01,"text":"Each answer gives you the next follow-up question:\n\nLucía: I moved here from Valencia."},{"start":272.75,"end":274.27,"text":"You: Really?"},{"start":275.33,"end":276.55,"text":"Why did you move here?"},{"start":276.55,"end":278.27,"text":"Lucía: For work."},{"start":278.93,"end":279.89,"text":"I work at a hotel."},{"start":280.39,"end":282.43,"text":"You: What do you do at the hotel?"},{"start":283.37,"end":289.09,"text":"In 2017, scientists at Harvard University studied conversations between strangers."},{"start":289.93,"end":293.09,"text":"People who asked more questions were liked more by their partners."},{"start":293.09,"end":295.77,"text":"Follow-up questions worked best."},{"start":296.41,"end":296.63,"text":"Why?"},{"start":297.37,"end":300.25,"text":"A follow-up question shows that you are really listening."},{"start":300.87,"end":303.07,"text":"But a good conversation goes both ways."},{"start":303.67,"end":307.35,"text":"If you never talk about yourself, people may think you are hiding something."},{"start":307.77,"end":309.85,"text":"So react to what you hear: Really?"},{"start":310.45,"end":310.91,"text":"Me too!"},{"start":311.37,"end":313.13,"text":"or That sounds fun!"},{"start":313.37,"end":316.69,"text":"Then tell something about yourself, and ask back."},{"start":317.81,"end":319.85,"text":"At 21:30, the exchange ends."},{"start":320.59,"end":321.61,"text":"Do not just walk away."},{"start":322.01,"end":323.51,"text":"The other person gave you their time."},{"start":323.97,"end":327.55,"text":"So thank them with kind words: It was nice to talk to you."},{"start":328.51,"end":329.63,"text":"Do you want to keep in touch?"},{"start":330.69,"end":331.45,"text":"Ask for WhatsApp."},{"start":332.41,"end":334.91,"text":"Keep in touch means you talk or write again later."},{"start":335.35,"end":339.05,"text":"In the Netherlands, WhatsApp is by far the most used app for messages."},{"start":339.51,"end":341.49,"text":"So almost everyone you meet can say yes."},{"start":342.03,"end":347.81,"text":"The exchange is every Thursday, so say goodbye for one week: See you next Thursday!"},{"start":348.45,"end":350.67,"text":"Next time, you can ask: How are you?"}],"words":[{"text":"A","start":0,"end":0.16},{"text":"language","start":0.16,"end":0.42},{"text":"exchange","start":0.42,"end":0.86},{"text":"is","start":0.86,"end":1.12},{"text":"a","start":1.12,"end":1.28},{"text":"trade","start":1.28,"end":1.6},{"text":"you","start":2.1,"end":2.2},{"text":"give","start":2.2,"end":2.36},{"text":"something","start":2.36,"end":2.72},{"text":"and","start":3.16,"end":3.32},{"text":"you","start":3.32,"end":3.42},{"text":"get","start":3.42,"end":3.56},{"text":"something","start":3.56,"end":3.88},{"text":"back","start":3.88,"end":4.18},{"text":"You","start":4.74,"end":4.86},{"text":"help","start":4.86,"end":5.08},{"text":